with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$Intrapleural pressure is normally:$x$, $x$Negative relative to atmospheric pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Negative relative to atmospheric pressure
The PLEURAL SPACE is the thin, fluid-filled gap between the two layers of pleura (visceral pleura covering the lung, parietal pleura lining the chest wall). The lungs naturally want to recoil inwards (elastic recoil), while the chest wall wants to spring outwards. These opposite pulls create a slight SUCTION in the pleural space, so INTRAPLEURAL PRESSURE is normally NEGATIVE relative to atmospheric pressure (about −4 to −5 cmH2O at rest, becoming more negative, about −8, during inspiration). This negative pressure keeps the lungs expanded against the chest wall.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal to atmospheric pressure — If intrapleural pressure became EQUAL to atmospheric pressure (e.g. when air enters through a wound), the lung would collapse — this is a pneumothorax.
• Positive relative to atmospheric pressure — POSITIVE intrapleural pressure occurs only during forced expiration (coughing) or in a tension pneumothorax, not normally.
• Highly variable with no consistent pattern — The pressure follows a consistent, predictable pattern with breathing.

⭐ Key point: A chest drain uses an underwater seal to let air out but not in, helping restore the negative pressure so the lung re-expands.$x$, null),
(2, $x$Which ECG change is classically associated with hypercalcemia?$x$, $x$Shortened QT interval$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
CALCIUM affects how quickly the heart muscle repolarises (recovers) after each beat. HYPERCALCAEMIA (high calcium) SPEEDS UP repolarisation, so the ST segment and QT interval become SHORT — a SHORTENED QT INTERVAL is the classic ECG sign. Very high calcium can also cause bradycardia, heart block and arrhythmias. (HYPOcalcaemia does the opposite — it PROLONGS the QT.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolonged QT interval — A PROLONGED QT is seen with HYPOcalcaemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and many drugs.
• Peaked T waves — PEAKED T waves are a sign of HYPERkalaemia.
• Prominent U waves — Prominent U waves are a sign of HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Common causes of hypercalcaemia in ICU: cancer (bone metastases, myeloma), hyperparathyroidism, immobilisation and thiazide diuretics. Treat with IV fluids (0.9% saline) and bisphosphonates.$x$, null),
(3, $x$How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the human spinal cord?$x$, $x$31$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 31
There are 31 PAIRS of spinal nerves, one pair leaving each segment of the spinal cord: 8 CERVICAL (C1–C8), 12 THORACIC (T1–T12), 5 LUMBAR (L1–L5), 5 SACRAL (S1–S5) and 1 COCCYGEAL (Co1). Total: 8 + 12 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 31. Each nerve carries both sensory and motor fibres. (Note there are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 — 12 is the number of pairs of CRANIAL nerves (and also the number of thoracic spinal nerve pairs).
• 24 — 24 is not the correct number.
• 43 — 43 is the total number of nerve pairs (31 spinal + 12 cranial), not spinal nerves alone.

⭐ Key point: Knowing the spinal levels helps nurses assess spinal injuries: e.g. C3–C5 (diaphragm), C5 (biceps), T4 (nipple line sensation), T10 (umbilicus), L4 (knee jerk), S1 (ankle jerk).$x$, null),
(4, $x$Which heart chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the entire systemic circulation?$x$, $x$Left ventricle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Left ventricle
The LEFT VENTRICLE receives OXYGENATED blood from the left atrium (which comes from the lungs through the pulmonary veins) and pumps it through the AORTA to the WHOLE BODY (the SYSTEMIC circulation). Because it must push blood against the high systemic pressure, it has the THICKEST muscular wall of all four chambers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right atrium — The right atrium receives DEOXYGENATED blood from the body through the venae cavae.
• Right ventricle — The right ventricle pumps DEOXYGENATED blood to the LUNGS through the pulmonary artery (low-pressure circuit).
• Left atrium — The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs and passes it to the left ventricle; it does not pump to the body.

⭐ Key point: Left ventricular failure causes blood to back up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema); right ventricular failure causes backup into the body (raised JVP, liver congestion, leg oedema).$x$, null),
(5, $x$The skin's appendages include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Alveoli$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
SKIN APPENDAGES (accessory structures) develop from the epidermis and include: HAIR FOLLICLES and hair, SWEAT GLANDS (eccrine and apocrine — temperature regulation), SEBACEOUS GLANDS (produce sebum to lubricate skin and hair), and NAILS. ALVEOLI are the tiny air sacs in the LUNGS where gas exchange takes place — they are NOT part of the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles ARE skin appendages.
• Sweat glands — Sweat glands ARE skin appendages.
• Sebaceous glands — Sebaceous glands ARE skin appendages.

⭐ Key point: In burns, deep partial-thickness wounds can heal from surviving appendages (hair follicles, sweat glands), but full-thickness burns destroy them and usually need skin grafting.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Keratinocytes make up approximately what percentage of the epidermis?$x$, $x$95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 95%
KERATINOCYTES are the main cell type of the EPIDERMIS (outer skin layer), making up about 90–95% of its cells. They produce KERATIN, a tough protein that makes the skin waterproof and protects against injury, infection and water loss. Keratinocytes are made in the deepest layer (stratum basale) and move upward, becoming flattened dead cells at the surface (stratum corneum), which are shed. Other epidermal cells (melanocytes, Langerhans cells, Merkel cells) make up the remaining small percentage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50% — 50% underestimates their dominance.
• 75% — 75% underestimates.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect because other cell types are also present.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the epidermis (e.g. burns, pressure injuries, TEN) removes this protective barrier, causing fluid loss and infection risk.$x$, null),
(7, $x$Urine passes through the renal collecting system in which correct sequence?$x$, $x$Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter
Urine is formed in the nephrons and flows through the collecting ducts into the renal papillae, then through: MINOR CALYX → MAJOR CALYX → RENAL PELVIS → URETER → bladder → urethra. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx; the major calyces join to form the renal pelvis, which narrows into the ureter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter — Major before minor calyx is the wrong order.
• Renal pelvis, major calyx, minor calyx, ureter — Renal pelvis before calyces is the wrong order.
• Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter — The renal pelvis comes after the major calyx.

⭐ Key point: Kidney stones can lodge at narrow points (pelvi-ureteric junction, pelvic brim, vesico-ureteric junction), causing severe colicky pain and obstruction.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Vital cardiorespiratory centres are located in the:$x$, $x$Medulla oblongata$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Medulla oblongata
The MEDULLA OBLONGATA, the lowest part of the brainstem, contains the VITAL CENTRES: the CARDIAC centre (controls heart rate), the VASOMOTOR centre (controls blood vessel tone and BP) and the RESPIRATORY centre (controls breathing rhythm). It also controls reflexes such as coughing, swallowing and vomiting. Damage (e.g. from brain herniation through the foramen magnum) causes respiratory arrest and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebellum — The cerebellum controls balance and coordination.
• Occipital lobe — The occipital lobe processes vision.
• Hypothalamus only — The hypothalamus controls temperature, hunger, thirst and hormones, but not the main cardiorespiratory centres.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP can compress the medulla, causing Cushing's triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) — a pre-terminal sign.$x$, null),
(9, $x$A rightward shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is caused by:$x$, $x$Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature
The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve shows how much oxygen haemoglobin holds at different oxygen levels. A RIGHTWARD SHIFT means haemoglobin holds oxygen LESS tightly and RELEASES MORE OXYGEN to the tissues. It is caused by INCREASED 2,3-DPG, INCREASED CO2, INCREASED TEMPERATURE, and ACIDOSIS (low pH) — conditions found in active tissues that need more oxygen (memory aid: "CADET, face Right" — CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased temperature — Decreased temperature shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Decreased CO2 — Decreased CO2 shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Alkalosis — Alkalosis shifts the curve to the LEFT.

⭐ Key point: Left shift (e.g. hypothermia, alkalosis, carbon monoxide, fetal haemoglobin) means haemoglobin holds oxygen tightly and releases less to tissues.$x$, null),
(10, $x$Haustra, the pouch-like segments visible along the colon, are formed largely due to the action of:$x$, $x$The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle
HAUSTRA are the pouch-like bulges seen along the COLON (large intestine). They are formed because the TENIAE COLI — three narrow bands of LONGITUDINAL smooth muscle running along the colon — are SHORTER than the colon wall, so they bunch it up into pouches (like gathering fabric with a drawstring). Haustra help mix and move faeces.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The circular muscle layer alone — The circular muscle layer alone does not create haustra.
• Villi projecting from the mucosa — Villi are found in the SMALL intestine (for absorption), not the colon.
• The greater omentum — The greater omentum is a fatty apron hanging from the stomach.

⭐ Key point: On abdominal X-ray, haustra (incomplete lines across the bowel) identify the LARGE bowel, whereas valvulae conniventes (complete lines) identify the SMALL bowel.$x$, null),
(11, $x$Residual volume is the amount of air:$x$, $x$Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration
RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the amount of air that REMAINS in the lungs after a MAXIMAL (forced, complete) EXPIRATION — about 1.2 litres in an average adult. It cannot be breathed out and keeps the alveoli from collapsing completely, allowing continuous gas exchange between breaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhaled during a normal breath — Air inhaled during a normal breath is the TIDAL VOLUME (about 500 mL).
• Exhaled forcefully after normal expiration — Air exhaled forcefully after a normal expiration is the EXPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 1.1 L).
• Inhaled during maximal inspiration beyond tidal volume — Air inhaled beyond the tidal volume is the INSPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 3 L).

⭐ Key point: Residual volume is increased in COPD and asthma (air trapping), and reduced in restrictive lung disease (e.g. fibrosis).$x$, null),
(12, $x$The short period that occurs during the transmission of impulses through the synapses is:$x$, $x$Synaptic delay$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Synaptic delay
SYNAPTIC DELAY is the short time (about 0.5 milliseconds) it takes for a nerve impulse to cross a chemical SYNAPSE. It includes the time for the neurotransmitter to be released from the first neuron, diffuse across the synaptic cleft, and bind to receptors on the next neuron. The more synapses in a pathway, the longer the total delay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Synaptic simulation — "Synaptic simulation" is not a physiological term.
• Synaptic junction — The synaptic junction is the structure where two neurons meet, not a time period.
• Synaptic cleft — The synaptic cleft is the physical GAP between neurons, not the time taken.

⭐ Key point: Reflex arcs with fewer synapses (e.g. the knee-jerk reflex — one synapse) are very fast.$x$, null),
(13, $x$The heart's four chambers consist of:$x$, $x$Two atria and two ventricles$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Two atria and two ventricles
The heart has FOUR CHAMBERS: TWO ATRIA (upper, thin-walled receiving chambers) and TWO VENTRICLES (lower, thick-walled pumping chambers). The right atrium and ventricle handle deoxygenated blood (to the lungs); the left atrium and ventricle handle oxygenated blood (to the body). Valves between them (tricuspid, mitral) and at the exits (pulmonary, aortic) keep blood flowing in one direction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Four ventricles only — Four ventricles only is incorrect.
• Four atria only — Four atria only is incorrect.
• One atrium and three ventricles — One atrium and three ventricles is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Blood flow order: body → right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.$x$, null),
(14, $x$The kidneys contribute to regulation of acid-base balance mainly by:$x$, $x$Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate
The KIDNEYS regulate ACID-BASE balance by EXCRETING HYDROGEN IONS (acid) into the urine (buffered by phosphate and ammonia) and by REABSORBING and GENERATING BICARBONATE (the body's main buffer). When the blood is too acidic, the kidneys excrete more acid and keep more bicarbonate; when too alkaline, they excrete bicarbonate. This "metabolic" compensation is slow (hours to days), unlike the lungs (minutes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing insulin — Insulin is produced by the pancreas.
• Regulating heart rate — Heart rate is controlled by the nervous system and hormones.
• Producing bile — Bile is produced by the liver.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, acid accumulates, causing metabolic acidosis — a common indication for dialysis.$x$, null),
(15, $x$Water reabsorbed by the collecting duct enters the:$x$, $x$Vasa recta$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vasa recta
In the COLLECTING DUCT, ADH (antidiuretic hormone) makes the walls permeable to water. Water moves out of the duct into the very concentrated tissue of the medulla and is then taken up by the VASA RECTA — the long, looping capillaries that run alongside the loops of Henle — which return the water to the general circulation. This allows the body to produce concentrated urine and conserve water.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephron loop — The nephron loop (loop of Henle) is part of the nephron, not where collecting duct water goes.
• Minor calyx — The minor calyx receives urine, not reabsorbed water.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: Without ADH (diabetes insipidus), the collecting duct stays impermeable to water, causing large volumes of dilute urine.$x$, null),
(16, $x$A nurse inadvertently infuses an IV solution containing potassium chloride too rapidly. Which of the following is an appropriate intervention advised by the physician?$x$, $x$Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution
Infusing POTASSIUM CHLORIDE too fast can cause dangerous HYPERKALAEMIA, which can lead to fatal arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. One recommended treatment is INSULIN added to 10% DEXTROSE. Insulin drives POTASSIUM INTO CELLS (by activating the sodium-potassium pump), quickly lowering blood potassium; the dextrose prevents hypoglycaemia. (Other urgent measures: STOP the infusion, give IV calcium gluconate to protect the heart, salbutamol, and remove potassium via binders or dialysis.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapid infusion of IV Ringer\'s solution — Ringer's solution contains potassium — it would not help.
• Rapid infusion of IV NS 0.9% — Normal saline does not lower potassium significantly.
• Administration of albumin — Albumin does not lower potassium.

⭐ Key point: Always give IV potassium by pump, diluted, and never faster than about 10 mmol/hr peripherally (20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).$x$, null),
(17, $x$Blood transfusion reactions can be classified broadly as:$x$, $x$Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions
BLOOD TRANSFUSION REACTIONS are broadly classified by timing: IMMEDIATE (ACUTE) reactions occur within 24 hours — acute haemolytic reaction (ABO incompatibility), allergic reactions (urticaria to anaphylaxis), febrile non-haemolytic reaction, TRALI (lung injury), TACO (fluid overload), and bacterial contamination (sepsis); DELAYED reactions occur days to weeks later — delayed haemolytic reaction, transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, post-transfusion purpura, and IRON OVERLOAD (with repeated transfusions).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only immediate reactions exist; delayed reactions are not possible — Delayed reactions do occur.
• Only allergic reactions occur — Many reaction types exist, not just allergic.
• Reactions cannot be classified in any meaningful way — Reactions are classified meaningfully, which guides management.

⭐ Key point: For any suspected reaction: stop the transfusion, keep the vein open with saline, check vital signs and patient ID, and inform the doctor and blood bank.$x$, null),
(18, $x$Type 1 Respiratory Failure is characterised by:$x$, $x$Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2
TYPE 1 (HYPOXAEMIC) RESPIRATORY FAILURE is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem with GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation), caused by V/Q mismatch or shunt — e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE. The CO2 is normal or low because the patient often breathes fast.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High PaCO2 only — High PaCO2 is TYPE 2 (hypercapnic) respiratory failure.
• Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 — Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.
• High PaO2 with low PaCO2 — High PaO2 with low PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = oxygenation problem (treat with oxygen, PEEP). Type 2 = ventilation problem (treat with ventilatory support).$x$, null),
(19, $x$To prevent graft-versus-host disease, blood is:$x$, $x$Irradiated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Irradiated
TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED GRAFT-VERSUS-HOST DISEASE (TA-GvHD) happens when donor T-lymphocytes (white cells) in transfused blood attack the recipient's tissues. It is rare but almost always fatal. IRRADIATING blood (gamma or X-ray) damages the DNA of donor lymphocytes so they cannot multiply, preventing TA-GvHD. Irradiated blood is given to high-risk patients: immunocompromised (e.g. stem cell transplant, Hodgkin lymphoma, certain chemotherapies), intrauterine and neonatal exchange transfusions, and transfusions from relatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leukocyte-reduced — Leukocyte reduction reduces febrile reactions and CMV transmission but does not reliably prevent TA-GvHD.
• Washed — Washing removes plasma proteins (for severe allergic reactions).
• Frozen — Freezing is used for long-term storage of rare blood.

⭐ Key point: Check that "irradiated" is specified on blood products when required.$x$, null),
(20, $x$A Modified Allen's Test that shows NO flushing after releasing the ulnar artery indicates:$x$, $x$Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site
The MODIFIED ALLEN'S TEST checks whether the ULNAR ARTERY can supply blood to the hand on its own (collateral circulation), in case the radial artery becomes blocked after puncture. Both arteries are compressed while the patient clenches the fist; the hand goes pale. When the ULNAR artery is released, colour (flushing) should return within about 5–15 seconds. NO FLUSHING means INADEQUATE COLLATERAL CIRCULATION — do NOT use that radial artery; choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safe to proceed with radial puncture — It is NOT safe to proceed if there is no flushing.
• The patient is dehydrated — It doesn't indicate dehydration.
• A normal, expected finding — No flushing is an abnormal finding.

⭐ Key point: Document the result of Allen's test before radial arterial puncture or line insertion.$x$, null),
(21, $x$The normal haemoglobin range for an adult male is approximately:$x$, $x$13.5–17.5 g/dL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 13.5–17.5 g/dL
The NORMAL HAEMOGLOBIN (Hb) range for an ADULT MALE is about 13.5–17.5 g/dL (135–175 g/L). For adult females it is about 12.0–15.5 g/dL. Haemoglobin carries oxygen in red blood cells. Low Hb (anaemia) reduces oxygen delivery to tissues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6–8 g/dL — 6–8 g/dL is severe anaemia.
• 20–24 g/dL — 20–24 g/dL is severe polycythaemia.
• 2–4 g/dL — 2–4 g/dL is life-threatening anaemia.

⭐ Key point: In most stable ICU patients, a restrictive transfusion threshold of Hb 7 g/dL (70 g/L) is used; higher in active bleeding or acute coronary syndrome.$x$, null),
(22, $x$George Kent is a 54-year-old widower with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and was rushed to the emergency department with increasing shortness of breath, pyrexia, and a productive cough with yellow-green sputum. He has difficulty communicating because of his inability to complete a sentence. One of his sons, Jacob, says he has been unwell for three days. Upon examination, crackles and wheezes can be heard in the lower lobes; he has tachycardia and a bounding pulse. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.3, PaCO2 68 mm Hg, HCO3 28 mmol/L, and PaO2 60 mm Hg. How would you interpret this?$x$, $x$Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated
STEP 1 — pH 7.3: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 68 mmHg is HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (COPD exacerbation — tired breathing muscles and airway obstruction cause CO2 retention). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 28 mmol/L is slightly HIGH: the kidneys have started compensating (retaining bicarbonate), but the pH is still below 7.35. Answer: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS, PARTIALLY COMPENSATED. PaO2 60 mmHg shows hypoxaemia. The infection (yellow-green sputum, fever) has triggered acute-on-chronic respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated — Metabolic alkalosis would show a high pH.
• Metabolic Acidosis, Partially Compensated — Metabolic acidosis would show a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated — Uncompensated would show a NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26).

⭐ Key point: Treat with controlled oxygen (target SpO2 88–92%), nebulised bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, and NIV if pH below 7.35.$x$, null),
(23, $x$Radial artery sampling for ABG is typically performed at what needle angle?$x$, $x$45°$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 45°
For RADIAL ARTERY ABG sampling, the needle is usually inserted at a 30–45 DEGREE angle, with the bevel up, facing against the blood flow. This angle allows the needle to enter the artery smoothly, which lies relatively superficially at the wrist. (For deeper arteries like the femoral, a steeper 60–90° angle is used.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90° — 90° is used for the femoral artery or intramuscular injections.
• 10° — 10° is too shallow and may miss the artery.
• 180° — 180° is not a possible insertion angle.

⭐ Key point: After sampling, apply firm pressure for at least 5 minutes (longer if anticoagulated) to prevent haematoma.$x$, null),
(24, $x$Haematological investigations typically assess:$x$, $x$Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters
HAEMATOLOGICAL investigations study the BLOOD and blood-forming tissues. They include: FULL BLOOD COUNT (haemoglobin, red cells, white cells and differential, platelets, haematocrit), blood film, COAGULATION tests (PT/INR, APTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer), reticulocyte count, iron studies, B12 and folate, blood group and crossmatch, and bone marrow examination.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only urine composition — Urine composition is assessed by urinalysis (renal/biochemistry).
• Only stool consistency — Stool consistency is a gastrointestinal assessment.
• Only skin pigmentation — Skin pigmentation is a dermatological assessment.

⭐ Key point: Daily FBC and clotting tests are common in ICU to monitor bleeding, infection, and drug effects (e.g. heparin).$x$, null),
(25, $x$Early ECG changes of hyperkalaemia typically include:$x$, $x$Tall, peaked T waves$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tall, peaked T waves
HYPERKALAEMIA affects heart conduction. EARLY ECG changes: TALL, PEAKED (tented) T WAVES. As potassium rises further: flattened or absent P waves, prolonged PR interval, WIDE QRS complexes, a "sine wave" pattern, and finally ventricular fibrillation or asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flattened T waves — Flattened T waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.
• Prolonged QT interval as the earliest change — Prolonged QT is not the earliest change of hyperkalaemia (it is seen with hypocalcaemia, hypokalaemia).
• U waves — U waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Treat severe hyperkalaemia: calcium gluconate (protect the heart), insulin-dextrose and salbutamol (shift potassium into cells), then remove potassium (binders, dialysis).$x$, null),
(26, $x$Potassium is most abundant in which compartment?$x$, $x$Intracellular$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intracellular
About 98% of the body's POTASSIUM is INSIDE the CELLS (INTRACELLULAR), at a concentration of about 140–150 mmol/L. Only about 2% is outside the cells (extracellular), where the normal blood level is 3.5–5.0 mmol/L. This large difference is maintained by the SODIUM-POTASSIUM PUMP and is essential for nerve and muscle function, including heart rhythm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extracellular — Extracellular fluid contains only a small amount.
• Intravascular — Intravascular (blood plasma) contains a small amount.
• Interstitial — Interstitial fluid contains a small amount.

⭐ Key point: Small shifts of potassium between cells and blood (e.g. from insulin, acidosis, cell breakdown) can cause big changes in blood levels.$x$, null),
(27, $x$The following are causes of respiratory acidosis EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hyperventilation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation
RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS is caused by HYPOVENTILATION — CO2 builds up because not enough is breathed out. Causes include COPD, severe asthma (exhaustion), head injury (depressed breathing centre), opioids, neuromuscular weakness, and chest wall problems. HYPERVENTILATION causes the OPPOSITE — CO2 is blown off, causing RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COPD — COPD IS a cause (CO2 retention).
• Severe asthma — Severe asthma IS a cause when the patient tires.
• Head injury — Head injury IS a cause (depressed respiratory drive).

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis = high PaCO2, low pH. Respiratory alkalosis = low PaCO2, high pH.$x$, null),
(28, $x$When starting a patient on opioids, the recommended protocol for preventing constipation is to:$x$, $x$Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener
OPIOIDS slow the movement of the gut (reduced peristalsis) and increase fluid absorption from the stool, causing CONSTIPATION in most patients, and tolerance to this side effect does NOT develop. The recommended prevention is a combination of a BOWEL STIMULANT (e.g. senna, bisacodyl — increases gut movement) and a STOOL SOFTENER (e.g. docusate, or an osmotic laxative such as lactulose or macrogol), started at the same time as the opioid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Add bulk fibre to the diet — Bulk fibre alone can worsen constipation with opioids (slow gut plus bulk can cause impaction).
• Give enemas as needed — Enemas "as needed" are reactive, not preventive.
• Increase fluids and exercise only — Fluids and exercise help, but are not enough alone.

⭐ Key point: For severe opioid-induced constipation, peripherally acting opioid antagonists (e.g. methylnaltrexone) can be used.$x$, null),
(29, $x$A patient presents with widened QRS complex, hypotension, and seizures after a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose. Which treatment is indicated to counteract the cardiotoxic sodium-channel blocking effect?$x$, $x$IV sodium bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: IV sodium bicarbonate
TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANTS (TCAs, e.g. amitriptyline) block SODIUM CHANNELS in the heart, which WIDENS the QRS complex and can cause ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension and seizures. IV SODIUM BICARBONATE (8.4%) is the treatment: the extra sodium overcomes the channel blockade, and the alkalosis (raised pH) reduces TCA binding to the sodium channels. Target blood pH 7.45–7.55.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil can cause seizures in TCA overdose and is contraindicated.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not TCAs.
• Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment — Activated charcoal may be given early (with a protected airway), but it does not treat established cardiotoxicity.

⭐ Key point: Treat TCA-induced seizures with benzodiazepines; avoid phenytoin and class Ia/Ic antiarrhythmics.$x$, null),
(30, $x$Bronchodilator nebulisers (e.g. salbutamol) can cause which common side effect that the nurse should monitor for?$x$, $x$Tachycardia and tremor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia and tremor
SALBUTAMOL (a beta-2 agonist) relaxes airway smooth muscle (bronchodilation). It also stimulates beta receptors elsewhere: in the heart, causing TACHYCARDIA and palpitations; in skeletal muscle, causing a fine TREMOR. It can also lower potassium (hypokalaemia) and raise glucose and lactate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and sedation — Bradycardia and sedation are not salbutamol effects.
• Hypoglycaemia — Salbutamol can RAISE blood glucose, not lower it.
• Weight gain — Weight gain is not a side effect.

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate, potassium and lactate in patients receiving frequent salbutamol nebulisers.$x$, null),
(31, $x$A patient with Torsades de Pointes is prescribed magnesium sulfate. Which ECG change confirms efficacy?$x$, $x$Shortened QT interval$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
TORSADES DE POINTES is a polymorphic VT associated with a PROLONGED QT interval. MAGNESIUM SULPHATE stabilises the heart cell membranes and suppresses the early after-depolarisations that trigger torsades. Its effectiveness is confirmed when the arrhythmia stops and the QT INTERVAL SHORTENS (returns towards normal), reducing the risk of recurrence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resolution of U waves — U waves relate to potassium, not the main target.
• Narrowed QRS complex — Narrowed QRS relates to sodium channel blockade (e.g. TCA overdose).
• Absent P waves — Absent P waves are seen in AF or hyperkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Also correct potassium (keep above 4.5 mmol/L) and stop QT-prolonging drugs.$x$, null),
(32, $x$A patient receiving IV vancomycin develops flushing, a rash on the upper body, and hypotension shortly after the infusion starts. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases
VANCOMYCIN infused TOO RAPIDLY can cause direct histamine release from mast cells — the "RED MAN" SYNDROME (now called vancomycin infusion reaction). Features: FLUSHING, itching and a red RASH on the face, neck and upper body, sometimes with HYPOTENSION. In most cases, it is related to the INFUSION RATE, not a true allergy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction only — It is not usually a true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction (though anaphylaxis is possible and must be excluded if there is wheeze or airway swelling).
• Normal expected drug effect requiring no action — It requires action (slowing or stopping the infusion).
• Sepsis unrelated to the medication — The timing with the infusion points to the drug, not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Stop or slow the infusion, give an antihistamine, and restart slowly (at least 60 minutes per gram).$x$, null),
(33, $x$Vasopressor infusions such as norepinephrine and dopamine should preferably be administered via:$x$, $x$A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis
VASOPRESSORS such as NORADRENALINE and DOPAMINE are powerful VASOCONSTRICTORS. If they leak out of a vein (EXTRAVASATION), they constrict the blood vessels in the surrounding tissue, causing TISSUE ISCHAEMIA and NECROSIS. A CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETER delivers the drug into a large vein with high blood flow, reducing this risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A peripheral IV in the hand for at least 24 hours before central access — Peripheral infusion for 24 hours increases the risk of extravasation injury; short-term peripheral use may be acceptable in emergencies with a large, well-placed cannula and close monitoring.
• Intramuscular injection — Vasopressors are not given IM.
• Oral route once the patient is hemodynamically stable — Vasopressors are not given orally.

⭐ Key point: If extravasation occurs, stop the infusion, aspirate if possible, and consider phentolamine infiltration.$x$, null),
(34, $x$10 year boys on admission with diagnosis of leukemia, which of these method/route of drugs administration will you avoid?$x$, $x$Intramuscular route$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intramuscular route
Children with LEUKAEMIA often have LOW PLATELETS (thrombocytopenia), so injections into muscle (INTRAMUSCULAR) can cause HAEMATOMAS (bleeding into the muscle). They also have LOW white cells (neutropenia), so IM injections increase the risk of INFECTION and abscesses. The IM route should be AVOIDED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral route — Oral is safe if the child can swallow.
• Intravenous route — IV (usually via central line) is the main route for chemotherapy and antibiotics.
• Sublingual route — Sublingual is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid rectal temperatures, enemas and suppositories in neutropenic or thrombocytopenic patients.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Dobutamine is primarily used in critical care for its:$x$, $x$Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output
DOBUTAMINE is mainly used for its INOTROPIC EFFECT — it stimulates β1 receptors in the heart, INCREASING CONTRACTILITY and CARDIAC OUTPUT. It is used in cardiogenic shock and low-output heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasoconstrictor effect — It is not a vasoconstrictor; it can cause mild vasodilation.
• Antiarrhythmic properties — It is not an antiarrhythmic; it can cause arrhythmias.
• Diuretic effect — It is not a diuretic (though improved output may increase urine).

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate and rhythm, BP and urine output during dobutamine infusion.$x$, null),
(36, $x$When titrating sedation in a mechanically ventilated ICU patient, the nurse should primarily use:$x$, $x$A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration
Sedation in ventilated ICU patients should be titrated using a VALIDATED SEDATION SCALE, such as the RICHMOND AGITATION-SEDATION SCALE (RASS) or Sedation-Agitation Scale (SAS). These give an objective target (often RASS −2 to 0 for light sedation), avoiding over-sedation (which prolongs ventilation and causes delirium) and under-sedation (agitation, self-extubation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's family's preference alone — Family preferences are important but cannot guide titration alone.
• No formal scale — sedation should be maximised at all times — Maximising sedation is harmful.
• Blood pressure alone as the only guide — BP alone is not a reliable guide to sedation depth.

⭐ Key point: Combine sedation scales with pain scales (CPOT, BPS) and delirium screening (CAM-ICU).$x$, null),
(37, $x$Which of the following is a thrombolytic agent?$x$, $x$Streptokinase$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Streptokinase
A THROMBOLYTIC (fibrinolytic) agent DISSOLVES EXISTING CLOTS by activating plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin. STREPTOKINASE is a thrombolytic (others: alteplase, tenecteplase, reteplase). Used in STEMI (when PCI is not available), massive PE, and acute ischaemic stroke (alteplase).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heparin — Heparin is an anticoagulant — it prevents new clots and growth, but doesn't dissolve existing clots.
• Clexane (enoxaparin) — Clexane (enoxaparin) is a low molecular weight heparin — an anticoagulant.
• Warfarin sodium — Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant.

⭐ Key point: Main risk of thrombolysis is bleeding, especially intracranial haemorrhage. Check contraindications carefully.$x$, null),
(38, $x$The preferred route for surgical antibiotic prophylaxis is:$x$, $x$Systemic (IV)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Systemic (IV)
SURGICAL ANTIBIOTIC PROPHYLAXIS is given by the SYSTEMIC (IV) route, usually within 60 MINUTES BEFORE the skin incision, so that high antibiotic levels are present in the tissues at the time of surgery. It reduces surgical site infections.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral — Oral absorption is unreliable and too slow for timing before surgery.
• Topical only — Topical antibiotics alone are not standard prophylaxis.
• Intramuscular only — IM is rarely used.

⭐ Key point: A repeat dose may be needed for long operations or major blood loss; prophylaxis is usually not continued beyond 24 hours.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Hypnotic drugs, as a pharmacological class, are primarily used to:$x$, $x$Induce and maintain sleep$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Induce and maintain sleep
HYPNOTIC drugs are used to INDUCE and MAINTAIN SLEEP. Examples: benzodiazepines (e.g. temazepam), "Z-drugs" (zolpidem, zopiclone), and melatonin agonists. In ICU, natural sleep promotion (lights off at night, noise reduction, eye masks, ear plugs) is preferred, and hypnotics are used cautiously because they can cause delirium and respiratory depression.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treat hypertension — Hypertension is treated with antihypertensives.
• Reverse anticoagulation — Anticoagulation is reversed with specific antidotes.
• Increase heart rate — Increasing heart rate is done with drugs like atropine.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypnotics in elderly patients where possible (falls, confusion).$x$, null),
(40, $x$The antidote for paracetamol overdose is:$x$, $x$Acetylcysteine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acetylcysteine
N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC, acetylcysteine) is the ANTIDOTE for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) OVERDOSE. In overdose, the liver's normal safe pathways are overwhelmed, and more paracetamol is turned into a toxic chemical called NAPQI. Normally, GLUTATHIONE in liver cells neutralises NAPQI, but in overdose glutathione runs out and NAPQI destroys liver cells (acute liver failure). NAC REPLENISHES GLUTATHIONE, protecting the liver. It is almost 100% protective if started within 8 hours of ingestion, and still helps when given later. Treatment decisions use the paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram (or treat immediately in staggered or late overdoses).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam), not paracetamol.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses OPIOIDS (e.g. morphine, heroin), not paracetamol.
• Atropine — Atropine treats ORGANOPHOSPHATE poisoning and symptomatic bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: NAC is given IV over 12–21 hours. It can cause anaphylactoid reactions (flushing, rash, wheeze) — slow or pause the infusion and give an antihistamine, then restart.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Which of the following is classified as a MAJOR/SEVERE contrast reaction?$x$, $x$Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest
CONTRAST REACTIONS are graded as mild, moderate or severe. SEVERE (major) reactions are life-threatening: LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway swelling), severe bronchospasm, profound hypotension, seizures, cardiac arrest and RESPIRATORY ARREST. These require immediate emergency treatment (adrenaline IM, oxygen, airway management, fluids, CPR).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild urticaria — Mild urticaria is a MILD reaction.
• Metallic taste — A metallic taste is a normal physiological effect.
• Mild nausea — Mild nausea is a MILD reaction.

⭐ Key point: Ask about previous contrast reactions, allergies, asthma and kidney function before giving contrast.$x$, null),
(42, $x$On imaging of intestinal obstruction, distinguishing small bowel from large bowel obstruction is aided by which feature?$x$, $x$Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)
On ABDOMINAL X-RAY, SMALL BOWEL loops show VALVULAE CONNIVENTES — mucosal folds that cross the ENTIRE WIDTH of the bowel (complete lines, like a "stack of coins"). LARGE BOWEL shows HAUSTRA — folds that do NOT cross the full width (incomplete lines). Small bowel is usually central; large bowel is peripheral (framing the abdomen).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is no radiological way to distinguish them — Plain X-ray can distinguish them.
• Only CT can ever differentiate them, never plain X-ray — Plain X-ray can be used; CT gives more detail.
• Large bowel is always more centrally located regardless of distension — Large bowel is usually peripheral, not central.

⭐ Key point: Upper limits of normal diameter: small bowel 3 cm, colon 6 cm, caecum 9 cm (the "3-6-9 rule").$x$, null),
(43, $x$Mediastinal change seen in massive pleural effusion on a chest radiograph$x$, $x$Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite
A MASSIVE PLEURAL EFFUSION fills the chest cavity with fluid, which PUSHES the MEDIASTINUM (heart, trachea) to the OPPOSITE side (away from the effusion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shift of mediastinum to the site of effusion — Shift towards the effusion occurs in lung collapse (loss of volume), not effusion.
• Central mediastinum — A massive effusion causes shift, not a central mediastinum.
• Tracheal shift to the site of lesion — The trachea shifts AWAY from a massive effusion.

⭐ Key point: Mediastinal shift AWAY: massive effusion, tension pneumothorax. TOWARDS: collapse, pneumonectomy.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Doppler ultrasound is particularly useful for assessing:$x$, $x$Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)
DOPPLER ULTRASOUND measures the movement of blood using the Doppler effect (sound waves change frequency when bouncing off moving blood cells). It is useful for assessing BLOOD FLOW and VASCULAR PATENCY: detecting DVT, arterial stenosis or occlusion, checking AV fistula and graft flow, and assessing fetal and placental blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bone density — Bone density is measured by DEXA scan.
• Lung consolidation — Lung consolidation is assessed by X-ray, CT, or lung ultrasound.
• Brain electrical activity — Brain electrical activity is measured by EEG.

⭐ Key point: Bedside Doppler is used to check peripheral pulses when they can't be felt.$x$, null),
(45, $x$The minimum amount of pleural fluid usually needed to be visible on a standard erect chest X-ray is:$x$, $x$150–300ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 150–300ml
On a standard ERECT (PA) chest X-ray, about 150–300 mL of pleural fluid is needed to blunt the COSTOPHRENIC ANGLE and be visible. Smaller amounts can be detected by lateral X-ray (about 50 mL), ultrasound (as little as 5–20 mL), or CT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10–50ml — 10–50 mL is too little to see on a standard PA film.
• 500–750ml — 500–750 mL is far more than the minimum.
• 1000–1500ml — 1,000–1,500 mL would cause obvious large opacity.

⭐ Key point: Bedside ultrasound is the most sensitive and practical tool for detecting and draining pleural fluid in ICU.$x$, null),
(46, $x$Which of the following is an example of secondary (long-term) storage?$x$, $x$Hard disk drive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hard disk drive
Computer memory is divided into PRIMARY memory (RAM, cache, registers — fast but TEMPORARY, lost when the power goes off) and SECONDARY storage (LONG-TERM, PERMANENT, keeps data when the power is off). A HARD DISK DRIVE (HDD) is secondary storage: it stores the operating system, programs and files (e.g. patient records) permanently on spinning magnetic disks. Other secondary storage: solid-state drives (SSD), USB flash drives, memory cards, optical discs (CD/DVD) and cloud storage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RAM — RAM (Random Access Memory) is PRIMARY memory — it holds what the computer is working on now and is erased when the power is switched off.
• CPU cache — CPU cache is very fast, small, temporary memory inside or near the processor.
• CPU register — CPU registers are tiny storage locations inside the processor that hold data being processed at that instant.

⭐ Key point: Always save work to secondary storage (and back it up) — unsaved data in RAM is lost during power cuts, which are common in many hospitals.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Why will computer-based patient records benefit clients more than paper records?$x$, $x$All of the options$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
COMPUTER-BASED patient records benefit clients more than paper records because: EASY RETRIEVAL of case files across facilities (records can be shared between hospitals and departments); BACKED-UP STORAGE protects data against loss (fire, flood, theft); LESS TIME spent finding files (instant search). So "ALL OF THE OPTIONS" is correct. Other benefits: legibility, decision support alerts, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Easy retrieval of case files across facilities — Easy retrieval is only one benefit.
• Backed-up storage protects data against loss (e.g. fire) — Backup is only one benefit.
• Less time spent retrieving case files — Time saving is only one benefit.

⭐ Key point: EHRs need strong security (passwords, access controls, audit trails) and downtime plans.$x$, null),
(48, $x$System software, as distinct from application software, is best described as:$x$, $x$Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)
SYSTEM SOFTWARE MANAGES the computer's HARDWARE (processor, memory, storage, devices) and PROVIDES A PLATFORM for other programs to run. The main example is the OPERATING SYSTEM (e.g. Windows, Linux, macOS), plus device drivers and utilities.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software written by end users for a single specific task — Software written for a specific task is APPLICATION software.
• Software used exclusively for entertainment — Entertainment software is a type of application software.
• A type of computer virus — A virus is malicious software, not system software.

⭐ Key point: Application software (e.g. EHR, Word) runs on top of system software.$x$, null),
(49, $x$An EHR audit trail is used to:$x$, $x$Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when
An EHR AUDIT TRAIL is an automatic record that TRACKS WHO ACCESSED or MODIFIED a patient's record and WHEN (including what was viewed or changed). It protects patient confidentiality, detects unauthorised access, and provides legal evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically diagnose patients — Audit trails do not diagnose patients.
• Replace nursing notes — They do not replace nursing notes.
• Calculate medication dosages — They do not calculate doses.

⭐ Key point: Only access the records of patients you are caring for — all access is recorded.$x$, null),
(50, $x$Random access file arrangement, compared to sequential access, allows:$x$, $x$Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records
RANDOM (DIRECT) ACCESS file organisation allows records to be accessed DIRECTLY (e.g. by a key or address) WITHOUT reading through all the preceding records. This is fast for finding individual records (e.g. one patient's file).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only reading records strictly in the order they were stored — Reading only in stored order is SEQUENTIAL access.
• No possibility of updating individual records — Random access allows updating individual records.
• Access only during scheduled batch windows — Random access is available at any time, not only in batch windows.

⭐ Key point: Databases often combine indexed and direct access for speed.$x$, null),
(51, $x$To increase SpO2 in a mechanical ventilated patient, which of the following parameters should be adjusted:$x$, $x$PEEP and FiO2$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PEEP and FiO2
OXYGENATION (SpO2, PaO2) on a ventilator is improved mainly by increasing the FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) and PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure). FiO2 increases the oxygen in each breath; PEEP keeps alveoli open (recruits collapsed alveoli), reducing shunt and improving oxygen transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fio2 and R/R — Rate mainly affects CO2.
• Vt and R/R — Tidal volume and rate mainly affect CO2.
• Vt and PEEP — Tidal volume affects CO2 more than oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Oxygenation = FiO2 + PEEP. Ventilation (CO2) = rate + tidal volume.$x$, null),
(52, $x$All of the following are synonyms for (or terms linked to) ARDS EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Acute liver injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute liver injury
ARDS (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome) is also known as or linked to: SHOCK LUNG syndrome (old name), DIFFUSE ALVEOLAR DAMAGE (the pathological finding), ACUTE LUNG INJURY (older term for milder forms), "wet lung" and "stiff lung". ACUTE LIVER INJURY is a LIVER condition, unrelated to ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock lung syndrome — Shock lung is an old name for ARDS.
• Diffuse alveolar damage — Diffuse alveolar damage is the typical pathology of ARDS.
• Acute lung injury — Acute lung injury was the older term for mild ARDS.

⭐ Key point: The Berlin definition (2012) replaced "acute lung injury" with mild ARDS.$x$, null),
(53, $x$1. Which One of the following is NOT a cause of acute ventilation failure?$x$, $x$Appendectomy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Appendectomy
ACUTE VENTILATORY FAILURE is caused by problems with the breathing "pump" or lungs: NEUROLOGICAL impairment (e.g. head injury, stroke, drug overdose depressing the breathing centre), NEUROMUSCULAR impairment (e.g. Guillain-Barré, myasthenia gravis, spinal cord injury), and PRIMARY RESPIRATORY problems (e.g. COPD, asthma, pneumonia). An APPENDECTOMY is a surgical procedure, not a cause of ventilatory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological impairment — Neurological impairment IS a cause.
• Neuromuscular impairment — Neuromuscular impairment IS a cause.
• Primary respiratory impairment — Primary respiratory impairment IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Post-operative patients can develop ventilatory failure from opioids, pain or atelectasis — but the surgery itself is not the cause.$x$, null),
(54, $x$All trauma patients undergoing emergency intubation should be treated as having a 'full stomach' primarily because:$x$, $x$Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk
All trauma patients needing emergency intubation are treated as having a "FULL STOMACH" because TRAUMA, PAIN, STRESS and OPIOIDS DELAY GASTRIC EMPTYING, so food or fluid may still be in the stomach many hours after the last meal. This greatly increases the risk of ASPIRATION during induction. Rapid sequence induction (RSI) with cricoid pressure (where used) is performed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They have usually eaten recently — The time since the last meal is often unknown and not the main reason.
• It simplifies documentation — It's not for documentation.
• It reduces the need for preoxygenation — Pre-oxygenation is still essential.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready and avoid bag-mask ventilation with high pressures (which inflates the stomach).$x$, null),
(55, $x$Which of the following is not a treatment modality for patient with epiglottitis?$x$, $x$Observation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Observation
EPIGLOTTITIS is a life-threatening swelling of the epiglottis that can suddenly block the airway. It always needs ACTIVE treatment, not simple observation. Treatment includes: SECURING THE AIRWAY (calling senior anaesthesia/ENT help; intubation in theatre, with a surgical airway — TRACHEOSTOMY or cricothyroidotomy — if intubation fails), IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. ceftriaxone, against Haemophilus influenzae type b and streptococci), STEROIDS (dexamethasone, to reduce swelling), humidified oxygen, and ICU monitoring. "OBSERVATION" ALONE is NOT a treatment modality — waiting without intervention risks sudden, complete airway obstruction and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tracheostomy — Tracheostomy (or cricothyroidotomy) IS used when the airway cannot be secured by intubation.
• Steroids — Steroids ARE used to reduce airway swelling.
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics ARE essential to treat the bacterial infection.

⭐ Key point: Do not lie the patient flat, do not examine the throat with a tongue depressor, and do not cause distress (e.g. cannulation) before airway experts arrive.$x$, null),
(56, $x$A nurse is assessing a patient with a known history of COPD. The nurse should be aware that the patient is at risk for Type II respiratory failure due to:$x$, $x$Chronic carbon dioxide retention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic carbon dioxide retention
Patients with COPD often have CHRONIC CARBON DIOXIDE RETENTION due to airway obstruction, air trapping, V/Q mismatch, and tired respiratory muscles. This puts them at risk of TYPE II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure, especially during exacerbations or with excessive oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An increase in gas exchange efficiency — COPD reduces gas exchange efficiency.
• A decrease in lung compliance — COPD (emphysema) often increases compliance; reduced compliance is more typical of fibrosis.
• A hyperactive respiratory drive — COPD patients do not have a hyperactive drive causing type II failure.

⭐ Key point: Target SpO2 88–92% in COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention.$x$, null),
(57, $x$A patient with a haemothorax and hypovolemic shock requires which priority intervention alongside chest tube drainage?$x$, $x$Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated
A HAEMOTHORAX with HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK means significant blood loss into the chest. Along with CHEST TUBE drainage (to remove blood and re-expand the lung), the priority is FLUID RESUSCITATION and BLOOD PRODUCT TRANSFUSION to restore circulating volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretic therapy — Diuretics would worsen hypovolaemia.
• Immediate extubation — Extubation is inappropriate in a shocked patient.
• Withholding IV access until surgery — IV access is essential for resuscitation.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax (over 1,500 mL initially or over 200 mL/hr for 2–4 hours) may need thoracotomy.$x$, null),
(58, $x$The most common trigger for an asthma exacerbation is:$x$, $x$Viral respiratory infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Viral respiratory infection
VIRAL RESPIRATORY INFECTIONS — especially RHINOVIRUS (common cold), influenza and RSV — are the MOST COMMON trigger of ASTHMA EXACERBATIONS in both children and adults (responsible for most attacks). The virus inflames the airway lining, increases mucus production and makes the airways more "twitchy" (hyper-reactive), causing bronchospasm. Exacerbations are more frequent in the rainy/cold season when viral infections spread.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Food allergy — Food allergy can trigger asthma in some people, but it is not the most common trigger.
• Exercise — Exercise can cause bronchoconstriction (exercise-induced asthma), but it rarely causes severe exacerbations requiring hospital care.
• Cold weather alone — Cold weather is a trigger, often because it increases viral infections, but it is not the most common trigger on its own.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: regular inhaled corticosteroids, annual influenza vaccination, hand hygiene, avoiding smoke and allergens, and a written asthma action plan.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Which are the Ventilator Parameters adjusted to maintain the optimum Minute Ventilation?$x$, $x$Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate
MINUTE VENTILATION (VE) is the total amount of air moved in and out of the lungs each minute: VE = TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) × RESPIRATORY RATE (RR). On a ventilator, VE is controlled by adjusting the TIDAL VOLUME and the RESPIRATORY RATE. Minute ventilation determines how much CO2 is removed: increasing Vt or RR lowers PaCO2; decreasing them raises PaCO2. Normal adult VE is about 5–8 L/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• FiO2 — FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) controls OXYGENATION, not minute ventilation.
• PEEP — PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure) improves OXYGENATION by keeping alveoli open; it does not set minute ventilation.
• Both A and B — "Both A and B" (FiO2 and PEEP) are both oxygenation controls, not ventilation controls.

⭐ Key point: Remember: OXYGENATION = FiO2 + PEEP; VENTILATION (CO2) = rate + tidal volume. Keep tidal volume lung-protective (6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight).$x$, null),
(60, $x$A 19-year-old comes into the emergency department with acute asthma. His respiratory rate is 44 breaths/minute, and he appears to be in acute respiratory distress. Which of the following actions should be taken FIRST?$x$, $x$Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.
A respiratory rate of 44/min with ACUTE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS in asthma is a medical emergency. The FIRST action is to GIVE a BRONCHODILATOR (e.g. salbutamol) by NEBULISER (with oxygen) to open the airways. Other actions (monitoring, history, emotional support) follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take a full medication history. — A full medication history delays treatment.
• Apply a cardiac monitor to the client. — A cardiac monitor is useful but not the first priority.
• Provide emotional support to the client. — Emotional support is important but doesn't treat the airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Also give oxygen (target SpO2 94–98%), ipratropium, and systemic steroids.$x$, null),
(61, $x$A normal PaO2 on arterial blood gas analysis is generally considered to be:$x$, $x$Above 80mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Above 80mmHg
PaO2 is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in ARTERIAL blood, measured on an arterial blood gas. On room air at sea level, the normal PaO2 is about 80–100 mmHg (10.5–13.5 kPa). It falls slightly with age. A PaO2 below 80 mmHg indicates HYPOXAEMIA, and below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) defines RESPIRATORY FAILURE — at this point SpO2 is about 90%, and further small falls in PaO2 cause large falls in oxygen saturation (the steep part of the dissociation curve).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Below 40mmHg — A PaO2 below 40 mmHg is severe, life-threatening hypoxaemia (similar to mixed venous blood).
• Exactly 20mmHg — 20 mmHg is incompatible with life for more than a short time.
• Above 200mmHg always required — A PaO2 above 200 mmHg is only seen with high supplemental oxygen; it is not "required" and very high levels may be harmful (oxygen toxicity).

⭐ Key point: Always note the FiO2 when interpreting PaO2 — use the P/F ratio (PaO2 ÷ FiO2) to judge severity of lung injury.$x$, null),
(62, $x$A patient recovering from thoracotomy has thick, tenacious secretions and a weak cough. The most appropriate nursing intervention is to:$x$, $x$Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy
After THORACOTOMY, pain limits deep breathing and coughing, so secretions build up, risking atelectasis and pneumonia. Nursing interventions: ADEQUATE ANALGESIA (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), DEEP BREATHING and COUGHING exercises with SPLINTING of the incision (pillow support), CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY, humidified oxygen (loosens secretions), early mobilisation, and incentive spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withhold analgesia to encourage a stronger cough — Withholding analgesia prevents effective coughing.
• Discourage any mobilisation — Mobilisation should be encouraged.
• Avoid humidified oxygen — Humidified oxygen helps thin secretions.

⭐ Key point: Epidural or paravertebral analgesia is often used after thoracotomy for good pain control.$x$, null),
(63, $x$What is the appropriate amount of time to suction a patient in a single interval?$x$, $x$No more than 10 – 15 seconds$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: No more than 10 – 15 seconds
Each SUCTION PASS should last NO MORE THAN 10–15 SECONDS. Longer suctioning removes oxygen from the lungs, causing HYPOXIA, bradycardia, arrhythmias, atelectasis and mucosal trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No more than 20 – 30 seconds — 20–30 seconds is too long.
• No more than 10 – 20 seconds — 10–20 seconds could be too long.
• No more than 5 – 10 seconds — 5–10 seconds may be too short to clear secretions effectively.

⭐ Key point: Pre-oxygenate before suctioning, apply suction only while withdrawing, and allow recovery between passes.$x$, null),
(64, $x$Pulse check location in an infant during CPR assessment is the:$x$, $x$Brachial artery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Brachial artery
In an INFANT (under 1 year), the BRACHIAL ARTERY (on the inside of the upper arm, between elbow and shoulder) is used to check the pulse during CPR. The infant's neck is short and fat, making the carotid hard to feel.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carotid artery — The carotid artery is used in children over 1 and adults.
• Radial artery — The radial artery is unreliable in shock and in infants.
• Femoral artery — The femoral artery can be used, but the brachial is standard.

⭐ Key point: Take no more than 10 seconds to check for a pulse; start CPR if not sure.$x$, null),
(65, $x$The primary hemodynamic benefit of IABP balloon inflation during diastole is:$x$, $x$Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) INFLATES during DIASTOLE (when the aortic valve closes), pushing blood back towards the heart and INCREASING CORONARY ARTERY PERFUSION (diastolic augmentation). It DEFLATES just before systole, reducing afterload and helping the heart eject blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased afterload — IABP DECREASES afterload (on deflation).
• Decreased myocardial oxygen supply — IABP INCREASES myocardial oxygen supply.
• Decreased diastolic pressure — IABP INCREASES diastolic pressure.

⭐ Key point: Timing is set using the ECG (inflate at the T wave/dicrotic notch, deflate before the R wave).$x$, null),
(66, $x$Hypertension is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease after which condition?$x$, $x$Diabetes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diabetes
DIABETES MELLITUS is the LEADING cause of END-STAGE RENAL DISEASE (ESRD — kidney failure needing dialysis or transplant) worldwide, accounting for about 40% of cases. HYPERTENSION is the SECOND leading cause. Both damage the small blood vessels and filtering units (glomeruli) of the kidneys over many years. In Nigeria and much of Africa, hypertension, diabetes and chronic glomerulonephritis are major causes of CKD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asthma — Asthma does not damage the kidneys.
• Hepatitis — Hepatitis B and C can cause certain kidney diseases (glomerulonephritis), but they are not leading causes of ESRD.
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis does not cause kidney failure (although frequent NSAID use for pain can).

⭐ Key point: Slow kidney damage by good control of blood glucose (HbA1c) and BP (often with ACE inhibitors or ARBs), and screen for protein in the urine.$x$, null),
(67, $x$What is the primary goal of a ventricular assist device (VAD) in critical care?$x$, $x$To support heart function and circulation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To support heart function and circulation
A VENTRICULAR ASSIST DEVICE (VAD) is a mechanical pump that SUPPORTS HEART FUNCTION and CIRCULATION when the heart is too weak to pump enough blood. It can be a bridge to recovery, to heart transplant, or long-term therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To monitor blood glucose levels — It doesn't monitor glucose.
• To provide mechanical ventilation — It doesn't provide mechanical ventilation.
• To administer antibiotics — It doesn't administer antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: VAD patients need anticoagulation and careful monitoring for bleeding, clots and infection.$x$, null),
(68, $x$A 37 year old male presented to the ER with chest pain, radiating to the left arm and diaphoresis. Upon history taking patient says that he has this problem for almost 3-4 hours and he has arrived here after a long drive. Assessing the patient's condition, the Emergency room Nurse diagnosis this patient as:$x$, $x$Emergent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Emergent
CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING (diaphoresis) for 3–4 hours suggests an ACUTE MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (heart attack). This patient needs immediate assessment and treatment — triaged as EMERGENT (life-threatening, needs immediate care). "Time is muscle" — every minute counts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Highly urgent — "Highly urgent" may underestimate the urgency.
• Urgent — "Urgent" suggests a delay is acceptable — it is not.
• Critically ill — "Critically ill" is a description, not a standard triage category here.

⭐ Key point: Obtain a 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival, give aspirin, and activate the cardiac team.$x$, null),
(69, $x$A 58-year-old man in the ICU suddenly complains of crushing chest pain radiating to his left arm, with diaphoresis and nausea. The nurse's FIRST priority action is to:$x$, $x$Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately
CRUSHING CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING and NAUSEA suggests an ACUTE CORONARY SYNDROME. The FIRST priority is to obtain a 12-LEAD ECG and VITAL SIGNS while ALERTING THE PHYSICIAN IMMEDIATELY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give the patient a glass of water and reassess in 30 minutes — Waiting 30 minutes delays treatment.
• Ask the patient to walk to the bathroom to see if pain resolves — Walking increases heart workload.
• Document the complaint and continue the routine round — Documenting and continuing the round ignores an emergency.

⭐ Key point: Give aspirin (if not contraindicated), oxygen if hypoxic, nitrates for pain (if BP allows), and prepare for reperfusion.$x$, null),
(70, $x$Which of these is not an antihypertensive$x$, $x$None of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: None of the above
All three drugs listed are ANTIHYPERTENSIVES: VALSARTAN — an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), which relaxes blood vessels; ATENOLOL — a beta-blocker, which slows the heart and reduces cardiac output; HYDRALAZINE — a direct arterial vasodilator, often used in pregnancy-related hypertension. Because every listed drug IS an antihypertensive, the correct answer is "NONE OF THE ABOVE" (none of them is a non-antihypertensive).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Valsartan — Valsartan IS an antihypertensive (ARB).
• Atenolol — Atenolol IS an antihypertensive (beta-blocker).
• Hydrallazine — Hydralazine IS an antihypertensive (vasodilator).

⭐ Key point: Main antihypertensive classes: ACE inhibitors (e.g. lisinopril), ARBs (e.g. losartan), calcium channel blockers (e.g. amlodipine, nifedipine), thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, and others (e.g. methyldopa, hydralazine, labetalol).$x$, null),
(71, $x$A recognised adverse effect of inotropic infusions (e.g. dobutamine) is:$x$, $x$Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia
INOTROPES such as DOBUTAMINE (and adrenaline, dopamine, milrinone) make the heart beat faster and harder. This INCREASES the heart's OXYGEN DEMAND. Recognised adverse effects: TACHYCARDIA and TACHYARRHYTHMIAS (e.g. atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopics, VT) and MYOCARDIAL ISCHAEMIA (chest pain, ST changes), especially in patients with coronary artery disease. Dobutamine can also cause hypotension (from β2 vasodilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia — Inotropes usually cause TACHYcardia, not bradycardia.
• Reduced myocardial oxygen demand — Inotropes INCREASE, not reduce, myocardial oxygen demand.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not a typical effect; adrenaline can actually cause HYPERglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: Use the lowest effective dose, monitor ECG continuously, and watch for new arrhythmias or chest pain. Correct potassium and magnesium to reduce arrhythmia risk.$x$, null),
(72, $x$Before delivering cardioversion, the nurse must confirm that:$x$, $x$The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode
SYNCHRONISED CARDIOVERSION is used for unstable tachyarrhythmias with a pulse (e.g. AF, atrial flutter, SVT, VT with a pulse). The shock must be timed to the R WAVE so it does not land on the T wave (the "vulnerable period"), which could trigger ventricular fibrillation. Before shocking, the nurse must confirm that "SYNC" mode is ON and the machine is SENSING (MARKING) each R WAVE on the monitor (usually shown as a dot or arrow above each QRS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The defibrillator is in unsynchronised mode — Unsynchronised mode is used for VF and pulseless VT, not cardioversion.
• Oxygen is flowing over the chest — Oxygen should be moved AWAY from the chest during shocks (fire risk), not flowing over it.
• The patient is awake and talking — The patient should be SEDATED (cardioversion is painful), not awake and talking.

⭐ Key point: Many defibrillators switch back to unsynchronised mode after each shock — re-select "sync" before every further cardioversion attempt.$x$, null),
(73, $x$A first-degree heart block on ECG is characterised by:$x$, $x$A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex
In FIRST-DEGREE HEART BLOCK, electrical impulses travel through the AV node more SLOWLY than normal, so the PR INTERVAL is PROLONGED (more than 0.20 seconds — five small squares). However, EVERY P WAVE is still FOLLOWED by a QRS complex — no beats are dropped. It is usually harmless and often seen with drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes — Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes is THIRD-DEGREE (complete) heart block.
• Absent P waves entirely — Absent P waves are seen in atrial fibrillation or junctional rhythms.
• A widened QRS with no P waves — A wide QRS with no P waves suggests a ventricular rhythm.

⭐ Key point: Second-degree block = some P waves not followed by QRS (Mobitz I with progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II with constant PR). Mobitz II and third-degree blocks are dangerous and may need pacing.$x$, null),
(74, $x$An ETCO₂ reading below what value after 20 minutes of CPR may prompt consideration of stopping resuscitation?$x$, $x$<10mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: <10mmHg
END-TIDAL CO2 (ETCO2) during CPR reflects the amount of blood the chest compressions are pushing through the lungs (cardiac output). Good-quality CPR usually produces an ETCO2 of at least 10–20 mmHg. An ETCO2 persistently BELOW 10 mmHg after about 20 MINUTES of good-quality CPR (in an intubated patient) suggests a very low chance of return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), and may be one factor in the team's decision to stop resuscitation. It should never be used alone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <25mmHg — 25 mmHg suggests reasonable blood flow during CPR — not a reason to stop.
• <40mmHg — 40 mmHg is a normal value (often seen after ROSC).
• <60mmHg — 60 mmHg is high.

⭐ Key point: A sudden rise in ETCO2 (e.g. to 35–40 mmHg) during CPR is often the first sign of ROSC. ETCO2 also confirms correct tube placement.$x$, null),
(75, $x$While caring for a client who has sustained an MI, the nurse notes eight PVCs in one minute on the cardiac monitor. The client is receiving an IV infusion of D5W and oxygen at 2 L/minute. The nurse’s first course of action should be to:$x$, $x$Notify the physician promptly.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Notify the physician promptly.
PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTIONS (PVCs) are extra beats from the ventricles. After an MI, FREQUENT PVCs (e.g. more than 6 per minute, multifocal, couplets or "R on T") show that the damaged heart muscle is electrically IRRITABLE, and may be a warning of VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA or VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION. Eight PVCs per minute is significant. The first action is to NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY, while assessing the patient (symptoms, BP, oxygen) and checking for causes (low potassium or magnesium, hypoxia, ischaemia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the oxygen concentration. — Increasing oxygen may help if hypoxic, but it does not address the possible arrhythmia risk on its own.
• Increase the IV infusion rate — Increasing the IV rate is not indicated and could cause fluid overload.
• Administer a prescribed analgesic. — Analgesia may be needed for pain, but it is not the first priority for frequent PVCs.

⭐ Key point: Have a defibrillator nearby, check electrolytes, and review the 12-lead ECG for ischaemia.$x$, null),
(76, $x$A client is brought to the emergency room complaining of pressure in her chest. Her blood pressure is 150/90, pulse 88, respirations 20. The nurse administers nitro-glycerine 0.4 mg sublingually as ordered. After five minutes her blood pressure is 100/60, pulse 96, respirations 20. Which of the following should the critical care nurse do next?$x$, $x$Document the results, and continue to monitor the client.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Document the results, and continue to monitor the client.
SUBLINGUAL GTN (nitroglycerin) dilates blood vessels, so a FALL in BP is EXPECTED. Here the BP fell from 150/90 to 100/60 — a systolic of 100 is still ACCEPTABLE (the usual concern is a systolic below 90 mmHg or a fall of more than 30 mmHg causing symptoms). The heart rate rose slightly (reflex). The nurse should DOCUMENT the results and CONTINUE to MONITOR the client (BP, pulse, pain level) and report as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Notify the physician that the client has become hypotensive, and obtain an order to administer IV fluids. — The client is not significantly hypotensive (systolic 100), so IV fluids are not needed.
• Place the client in semi-Fowler’s position, and administer O2 at 4 L. — Positioning and oxygen may be part of care, but are not the specific next step indicated by these findings.
• Administer a second dose of nitro-glycerine. — A second dose may be considered if chest pain continues, but with BP now 100/60 it should be given cautiously only after reassessing pain and BP.

⭐ Key point: Before each GTN dose, check BP (hold if systolic below 90 mmHg) and ask about sildenafil/tadalafil use.$x$, null),
(77, $x$Which mnemonic is commonly used to remember indications for urgent or emergent dialysis?$x$, $x$AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia
The AEIOU mnemonic lists indications for URGENT/EMERGENCY DIALYSIS when medical treatment fails: A = ACIDOSIS (severe metabolic acidosis, e.g. pH below 7.1–7.15); E = ELECTROLYTE imbalance (especially refractory HYPERKALAEMIA); I = INTOXICATION/INGESTION (dialysable poisons such as lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates); O = OVERLOAD (fluid overload/pulmonary oedema not responding to diuretics); U = URAEMIA (uraemic pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SEPSIS — SEPSIS relates to sepsis recognition.
• MONA — MONA (Morphine, Oxygen, Nitrates, Aspirin) relates to acute coronary syndrome treatment.
• FAST — FAST relates to stroke recognition.

⭐ Key point: Dialysis decisions are based on the patient's clinical state, not a single creatinine value.$x$, null),
(78, $x$Which finding is most suggestive of contrast-induced nephropathy after coronary angiography?$x$, $x$Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure
CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (contrast-associated AKI) is a decline in kidney function after IV contrast (e.g. coronary angiography or CT). It is diagnosed by a RISE in SERUM CREATININE (e.g. ≥26.5 µmol/L or ≥25% above baseline) within 24–72 HOURS of contrast exposure. It usually peaks at 3–5 days and often recovers within 1–3 weeks. Risk factors: CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure, large contrast volume, and nephrotoxic drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperkalemia occurring within minutes of injection — Hyperkalaemia within minutes is not typical.
• Gross hematuria developing after two weeks — Gross haematuria after two weeks is not typical.
• Immediate anuria during the procedure — Immediate anuria during the procedure is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: identify at-risk patients, ensure adequate IV hydration (e.g. 0.9% saline before and after), use the lowest contrast dose, and hold nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs, metformin in severe CKD).$x$, null),
(79, $x$A patient's urine output is measured at 20ml over the last hour, with a weight of 70kg. This represents a urine output of approximately:$x$, $x$0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment
URINE OUTPUT in mL/kg/hr = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL ÷ 1 hour ÷ 70 kg = 0.29, which is approximately 0.3 mL/kg/hr. The minimum normal adult urine output is 0.5 mL/kg/hr. Below this is OLIGURIA, which requires assessment (check catheter patency, fluid status, BP, and causes of AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2ml/kg/hr, a normal finding — 2 mL/kg/hr would be 140 mL/hr for this patient.
• 5ml/kg/hr, indicating polyuria — 5 mL/kg/hr would be 350 mL/hr (polyuria).
• No clinical significance regardless of the value — Urine output is always clinically significant in ICU.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria for 6 hours or more meets KDIGO criteria for AKI. Always check that the catheter is not blocked before assuming kidney failure.$x$, null),
(80, $x$A patient with a very dry mouth, skin, and mucous membranes is diagnosed with dehydration. Which intervention should the nurse perform when caring for a patient diagnosed with fluid volume deficit?$x$, $x$Assessing urinary intake and output$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assessing urinary intake and output
In FLUID VOLUME DEFICIT (dehydration), the nurse should ASSESS URINARY INTAKE and OUTPUT accurately (and other signs: vital signs, skin turgor, mucous membranes, daily weights, urine colour and specific gravity). Intake and output show whether the patient is receiving enough fluid and whether the kidneys are responding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtaining the clients weight weekly at different times of the day. — Weight should be measured DAILY, at the SAME time, with the same scale and clothing — not weekly at different times.
• Maintaining I V therapy at the keep vein open rate. — IV fluid at a "keep vein open" rate is too slow to correct dehydration.
• Monitoring arterial blood gas ABG results — ABGs are not the main monitoring tool for simple dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Signs of dehydration: thirst, dry mucous membranes, low urine output, concentrated urine, tachycardia, hypotension, and weight loss.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Acute kidney injury caused by renal artery stenosis reducing blood flow to the kidney is classified as:$x$, $x$Prerenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
AKI is classified by where the problem is: PRE-RENAL (reduced blood flow TO the kidney), INTRINSIC/RENAL (damage WITHIN the kidney tissue), or POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow AFTER the kidney). RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS narrows the artery supplying the kidney, REDUCING BLOOD FLOW, so it is classified as PRE-RENAL (it affects perfusion before blood reaches the kidney tissue). ACE inhibitors can precipitate AKI in bilateral renal artery stenosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intrinsic (renal) — Intrinsic AKI involves direct damage to the kidney tissue (e.g. ATN, glomerulonephritis).
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI involves obstruction of the urinary tract.
• Not a cause of AKI — Renal artery stenosis IS a cause of AKI.

⭐ Key point: Pre-renal AKI usually improves quickly if blood flow is restored before the tubules are damaged.$x$, null),
(82, $x$A patient with severe hemorrhage and hypotension develops oliguria and rising creatinine. This mechanism of acute kidney injury is best classified as:$x$, $x$Prerenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
SEVERE HAEMORRHAGE and HYPOTENSION reduce blood flow to the kidneys. The kidneys respond by retaining sodium and water, producing a small amount of concentrated urine (OLIGURIA), and the creatinine rises. This is PRE-RENAL AKI. If hypoperfusion is prolonged, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephrotoxic — Nephrotoxic AKI is caused by drugs or toxins damaging the kidney.
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal (intrinsic) AKI involves direct kidney damage.

⭐ Key point: Treat with prompt fluid and blood resuscitation and control of bleeding.$x$, null),
(83, $x$What is the standard approach to bladder management for an unconscious patient?$x$, $x$Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring
An UNCONSCIOUS patient cannot void voluntarily and is at risk of urinary retention, incontinence (skin damage) and inaccurate fluid assessment. The standard approach is INDWELLING URINARY CATHETER placement with STRICT INTAKE AND OUTPUT MONITORING (hourly urine output in ICU). Use aseptic insertion and catheter care, and remove the catheter as soon as it is no longer needed to reduce catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No catheterization is needed for an unconscious patient — Catheterisation is usually needed for accurate monitoring and to prevent retention.
• Encouraging the patient to void on command — An unconscious patient cannot void on command.
• Fluid restriction — Fluid restriction is not the standard approach.

⭐ Key point: CAUTI bundle: aseptic insertion, closed drainage, bag below bladder level, daily review of need.$x$, null),
(84, $x$Diazepam, as an alternative first-line agent for status epilepticus, can be given via all of the following routes EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Oral tablet during active seizure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oral tablet during active seizure
DIAZEPAM can be given for status epilepticus via: IV, RECTAL (e.g. rectal tubes), and sometimes buccal/nasal (though buccal/nasal midazolam is more common). It should NOT be given as an ORAL TABLET during an active seizure — the patient cannot swallow safely (choking and aspiration risk) and absorption would be too slow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV — IV is a fast route for diazepam.
• Rectal — Rectal diazepam is used, especially in children and outside hospital.
• Buccal or nasal — Buccal or nasal routes are used (mainly midazolam).

⭐ Key point: Never put anything in the mouth of a fitting patient.$x$, null),
(85, $x$What is the recommended external anatomical reference point used to zero and level an external ventricular drain (EVD)?$x$, $x$Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)
An EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN (EVD) is levelled and zeroed at the level of the FORAMEN OF MONRO (the opening connecting the lateral and third ventricles). The external landmark is the TRAGUS of the ear (the small cartilage flap in front of the ear canal), or the outer canthus of the eye in some units.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Xiphoid process — The xiphoid process is not used.
• Umbilicus — The umbilicus is not used.
• Sternal angle — The sternal angle is used for JVP assessment, not EVD.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the EVD every time the patient's head position changes; wrong levelling causes over- or under-drainage of CSF.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Meningeal syndrome includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Babinski response$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Babinski response
MENINGEAL SYNDROME (meningism) is caused by irritation of the meninges (e.g. meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). It includes: NECK STIFFNESS, HEADACHE, PHOTOPHOBIA (sensitivity to light), nausea/vomiting, and positive KERNIG'S and BRUDZINSKI'S signs. A BABINSKI RESPONSE (upgoing big toe) is a sign of UPPER MOTOR NEURONE damage (brain or spinal cord), not meningeal irritation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Headache — Headache IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Photophobia — Photophobia IS part of meningeal syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Kernig's sign: pain on extending the knee with the hip flexed. Brudzinski's sign: neck flexion causes hip and knee flexion.$x$, null),
(87, $x$The nurse is assessing the pupils of an unconscious patient and notes that they are unequal in size. This finding is known as:$x$, $x$Anisocoria$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anisocoria
ANISOCORIA means UNEQUAL PUPIL SIZES. In an unconscious patient, a new anisocoria (especially a dilated, sluggish pupil) may indicate compression of the third cranial nerve by a mass or raised ICP (e.g. uncal herniation) — a neurosurgical emergency. A small degree of anisocoria (less than 1 mm) can be normal in some people.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Miosis — Miosis means small (constricted) pupils.
• Mydriasis — Mydriasis means large (dilated) pupils.
• Hippus — Hippus is rhythmic pupil constriction and dilation.

⭐ Key point: Report any new pupil inequality immediately in neurological patients.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Which does NOT need ruled out before assessing for brain death?$x$, $x$Fractures$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fractures
Before testing for BRAIN DEATH, REVERSIBLE causes of coma must be EXCLUDED, because they can mimic brain death: ELECTROLYTE and METABOLIC imbalances (e.g. severe hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia), HYPOTHERMIA (core temperature must be above about 34–36 °C), DRUG INTOXICATION (sedatives, opioids, muscle relaxants), and endocrine disorders. FRACTURES do not affect brainstem function and do not need to be ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Electrolyte imbalances — Electrolyte imbalances must be ruled out.
• Hypothermia — Hypothermia must be ruled out.
• Drug intoxication — Drug intoxication must be ruled out.

⭐ Key point: Brain death testing is done by two senior doctors and includes brainstem reflexes and an apnoea test.$x$, null),
(89, $x$A Glasgow Coma Scale score in which range classifies a traumatic brain injury as severe?$x$, $x$3-8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 3-8
TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI) severity is classified using the GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) after resuscitation: MILD = 13–15; MODERATE = 9–12; SEVERE = 3–8. A GCS of 3–8 means the patient is in a coma, usually cannot protect the airway (needs intubation), and needs ICU care, CT scan and often ICP monitoring. Severe TBI carries a high risk of death and long-term disability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 13-15 — 13–15 is MILD TBI.
• 9-12 — 9–12 is MODERATE TBI.
• 3-5 only — "3–5 only" is too narrow; severe TBI includes GCS 3 to 8.

⭐ Key point: Prevent secondary brain injury: avoid hypoxia (SpO2 above 94%), hypotension (systolic above 100–110 mmHg), hypo/hypercapnia, fever, hypo/hyperglycaemia and seizures.$x$, null),
(90, $x$Which statement about delirium and dementia is TRUE?$x$, $x$Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive
DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention and awareness, usually caused by a medical condition (infection, drugs, hypoxia, metabolic problems). It is often REVERSIBLE when the cause is treated. DEMENTIA is a CHRONIC, PROGRESSIVE decline in memory and thinking over months to years, and is usually NOT reversible. Patients with dementia are at higher risk of delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delirium is never reversible — Delirium is often reversible.
• Dementia has an acute onset over hours — Dementia has a gradual, not acute, onset.
• Delirium and dementia are the same condition — They are different conditions.

⭐ Key point: Screen ICU patients for delirium regularly using CAM-ICU or ICDSC.$x$, null),
(91, $x$What is the primary purpose of the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) in critical care?$x$, $x$C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) is a standard tool to EVALUATE NEUROLOGICAL STATUS and LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS, using three components: eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5), and motor response (1–6). Total 3–15. It helps detect changes over time and guides decisions (e.g. intubation when GCS ≤8).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring blood pressure — It doesn't monitor BP.
• B) Assessing respiratory function — It doesn't assess respiratory function directly.
• D) Administering antibiotics — It isn't related to antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: Always report the individual components (E, V, M) as well as the total.$x$, null),
(92, $x$Skeletal muscles disorder commonly occurs following sepsis, mobility restriction, hyperglycemia and neuromuscular blocking agents is termed$x$, $x$Critical care acquired weakness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critical care acquired weakness
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (critical care-acquired weakness) is generalised, symmetrical muscle weakness developing during critical illness. It includes CRITICAL ILLNESS POLYNEUROPATHY (nerve damage), CRITICAL ILLNESS MYOPATHY (muscle damage), or both. Risk factors: SEPSIS, multi-organ failure, IMMOBILITY, HYPERGLYCAEMIA, NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS and steroids. The broad term for the clinical syndrome is ICU-acquired weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Critical illness polyneuropathy — Critical illness polyneuropathy is one type (nerve).
• Critical illness myopathy — Critical illness myopathy is one type (muscle).
• Critical illness neuropathy — "Critical illness neuropathy" is not the overall term.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early mobilisation, glucose control, light sedation and minimising neuromuscular blockers.$x$, null),
(93, $x$What is the priority nursing intervention for airway management in an unconscious patient?$x$, $x$Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway
An UNCONSCIOUS patient may lose airway reflexes; the tongue can fall back and block the airway, and vomit or secretions can be aspirated. Positioning the patient LATERALLY (RECOVERY POSITION) or semi-prone (with the head of bed elevated where appropriate) helps keep the AIRWAY PATENT, allows secretions to drain from the mouth, and reduces aspiration risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Position the patient supine and flat — Supine and flat increases airway obstruction and aspiration risk.
• Insert an oral airway only if the gag reflex is present — Oral airways are inserted only if the gag reflex is ABSENT (otherwise they cause gagging and vomiting).
• Elevate the legs above the level of the heart — Elevating the legs doesn't protect the airway.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready, and consider airway adjuncts or intubation if the airway cannot be maintained (GCS ≤8).$x$, null),
(94, $x$A patient has a seizure with uncontrollable rhythmic jerking movements. This is documented as a:$x$, $x$Clonic seizure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clonic seizure
A CLONIC SEIZURE involves RHYTHMIC, REPETITIVE JERKING movements of the muscles, caused by repeated cycles of muscle contraction and relaxation. In a generalised TONIC-CLONIC seizure, the patient first stiffens (tonic phase, often with a cry and cyanosis), then has rhythmic jerking (clonic phase), followed by a post-ictal period of drowsiness and confusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tonic seizure — A TONIC seizure involves sustained STIFFENING of muscles, without rhythmic jerking.
• Absence seizure — An ABSENCE seizure is a brief (seconds) loss of awareness with staring, mostly in children.
• Myoclonic seizure — A MYOCLONIC seizure involves sudden, brief, shock-like single jerks, not continuous rhythmic jerking.

⭐ Key point: During a seizure: time it, protect the head, do not restrain or put anything in the mouth, give oxygen, check glucose, and turn to the recovery position afterwards. Seizures longer than 5 minutes need benzodiazepines.$x$, null),
(95, $x$Which statement about the unconscious patient is NOT correct?$x$, $x$A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function
In UNCONSCIOUS patients, SpO2 shows only OXYGENATION, NOT VENTILATION. A patient can have normal SpO2 (especially on oxygen) while CO2 is rising dangerously (hypoventilation). Full respiratory assessment requires observing breathing rate, depth and pattern, airway patency, chest movement, and ABGs or capnography. So the statement that SpO2 provides "all the information" is NOT correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory failure can cause unconsciousness — Respiratory failure CAN cause unconsciousness (hypoxia, high CO2).
• Poor conscious levels are often accompanied by altered breathing patterns — Altered breathing patterns DO accompany poor consciousness.
• Airway protection is a nursing priority — Airway protection IS a nursing priority.

⭐ Key point: Use capnography to monitor ventilation in sedated or unconscious patients.$x$, null),
(96, $x$Delirium Risk Factors are as follows EXCEPT$x$, $x$Obesity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Obesity
DELIRIUM is an acute brain dysfunction with many risk factors. PREDISPOSING factors: older age, PRE-EXISTING DEMENTIA or cognitive impairment, depression, alcohol use, visual/hearing impairment, severe illness. PRECIPITATING factors: INFECTIONS (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), surgery and BONE FRACTURES (e.g. hip fracture), hypoxia, metabolic disturbances, drugs (benzodiazepines, opioids, anticholinergics), dehydration, urinary retention, constipation, pain, sleep deprivation and immobility. OBESITY is NOT a recognised major risk factor for delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preexisting dementia — Pre-existing dementia IS one of the strongest risk factors.
• Bone fractures — Bone fractures (especially hip fractures in older people) ARE a risk factor.
• Infections — Infections ARE a common precipitating factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevention bundles (reorientation, sleep promotion, early mobility, hearing aids/glasses, hydration, avoiding deliriogenic drugs) reduce delirium by about a third.$x$, null),
(97, $x$An effective nursing strategy to reduce ICU delirium includes:$x$, $x$Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos
REORIENTATION using a CLOCK, CALENDAR, FAMILY PHOTOS, explanations of where the patient is and what is happening, glasses and hearing aids, helps reduce ICU DELIRIUM. Other strategies: consistent caregivers, natural day–night light cycles, reducing noise at night, early mobilisation, and family involvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequent changes of caregiving staff — Frequent staff changes increase confusion.
• Keeping the environment brightly lit and noisy at all times — Bright, noisy environments disrupt sleep and worsen delirium.
• Withholding explanations to avoid overstimulation — Explanations help orientation and reduce anxiety.

⭐ Key point: The ABCDEF bundle reduces delirium.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient with Parkinson’s disease and delirium should avoid:$x$, $x$Haloperidol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haloperidol
PARKINSON'S DISEASE is caused by a loss of DOPAMINE-producing cells in the brain. HALOPERIDOL is a typical antipsychotic that strongly BLOCKS DOPAMINE (D2) receptors. In a patient with Parkinson's, it can severely WORSEN rigidity, tremor and immobility, cause falls and swallowing problems, and may trigger neuroleptic malignant syndrome. It should be AVOIDED. If an antipsychotic is absolutely needed for distressing delirium, QUETIAPINE (weak dopamine blockade) is preferred at low doses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quetiapine — Quetiapine is relatively safe in Parkinson's disease and may be used if needed.
• Melatonin — Melatonin may help regulate sleep and is safe.
• Dexmedetomidine — Dexmedetomidine (an alpha-2 agonist sedative) does not block dopamine and is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid metoclopramide and prochlorperazine in Parkinson's patients; use domperidone for nausea. Never stop Parkinson's medications abruptly (risk of a parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome).$x$, null),
(99, $x$What percentage of mechanically ventilated ICU patients may experience delirium?$x$, $x$Up to 80%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Up to 80%
DELIRIUM is very common in ICU, especially in MECHANICALLY VENTILATED patients — studies report it in up to about 60–80%. Contributing factors: severe illness (sepsis), sedative drugs (especially benzodiazepines), sleep deprivation, immobility, hypoxia, metabolic problems, and the ICU environment. Much ICU delirium is the "HYPOACTIVE" type (quiet, withdrawn, drowsy), which is easily missed without routine screening. Delirium is linked to longer ventilation, longer stay, higher mortality and long-term cognitive impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Up to 10% — 10% greatly underestimates the problem.
• Up to 30% — 30% underestimates it for ventilated patients.
• 0% — 0% is incorrect — delirium is very common.

⭐ Key point: Screen every shift with CAM-ICU or ICDSC, and use the ABCDEF bundle to prevent and manage delirium.$x$, null),
(100, $x$Post-ICU delirium may lead to:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Surviving critical illness, especially with delirium, can lead to long-term problems known as POST-INTENSIVE CARE SYNDROME (PICS): LONG-TERM COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT (memory, attention and problem-solving difficulties — sometimes like mild dementia), mental health problems including PTSD (flashbacks and nightmares of ICU experiences), anxiety and depression, and FUNCTIONAL DECLINE (weakness, difficulty with daily activities, inability to return to work). So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Family members can also suffer (PICS-Family).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Long-term cognitive impairment — Long-term cognitive impairment is only one effect.
• PTSD — PTSD is only one effect.
• Functional decline — Functional decline is only one effect.

⭐ Key point: ICU diaries, early rehabilitation, family involvement, and ICU follow-up clinics help patients and families recover.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
select count(distinct n) as "questions updated (should be 100)", count(*) as "rows changed" from upd;
